WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.340
Mice that lack a cold-and-menthol channel were trained to report gentle warming of a

2
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.340
forepaw.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.980
They failed completely.

4
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.780
Why would a cold channel matter for warmth?

5
00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:21.483
A transducer turns a stimulus into an electrical signal.

6
00:00:22.263 --> 00:00:24.603
For temperature, the candidates are ion channels.

7
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:29.087
A line is one kind of sensory fibre and what it reports.

8
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:31.227
A channel sits in a line.

9
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.500
Most fibres in this story are C fibres.

10
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.500
They are thin, unmyelinated and slow.

11
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:42.873
For gentle cooling, the main established transducer is TRPM8.

12
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:48.333
A fibre that is silent at rest can report a change only by starting to fire.

13
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:55.247
In 2020, one study recorded single fibres from mouse forepaw skin kept alive with

14
00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:56.247
its nerve.

15
00:00:56.717 --> 00:01:00.057
Awake forepaw skin measured 26 to 28 degrees.

16
00:01:00.937 --> 00:01:02.797
So they also used a bath at 27.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.640
Suppose a fibre already fires slowly at rest.

18
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.400
What can it report that a silent fibre cannot?

19
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:17.753
It can report change in both directions.

20
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:20.533
Faster for one, slower for the other.

21
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:25.573
In the 27 degree bath, one population did exactly that.

22
00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:28.033
These were cool-sensitive C fibres.

23
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:32.053
With no stimulus, they fired slowly and steadily.

24
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.693
Cooling makes such fibres fire faster.

25
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:37.407
Warming made them fire slower.

26
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.730
A second kind, warm-excited C fibres, had little ongoing firing.

27
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.070
Warming made them fire faster.

28
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.090
In the usual 32 degree bath, no such fibres had been seen.

29
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.620
Semicircular canal fibres in the inner ear use a similar design.

30
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.160
They fire at rest, so rotation can speed them up or slow them down.

31
00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:09.533
The one idea: skin temperature is read from a few lines at once, not one channel per

32
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:10.533
feeling.

33
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:13.983
A line that fires at rest can signal both ways.

34
00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:17.463
The cool line speeds up on cooling and slows on warming.

35
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:23.213
So a channel that supplies a line's firing matters for everything that reads that

36
00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:26.953
line. That is how losing a cool channel can cost warmth.

37
00:02:27.966 --> 00:02:29.626
This is a mouse forepaw result.

38
00:02:30.426 --> 00:02:33.286
The circuit that reads the lines has not been identified.

39
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.260
The picture to keep: a fibre that fires at rest can say warmer by going quiet.
